Run calc_ionograms.py under MPI, like detect_chirps.py already is.

`calc_ionograms.py` is the only process on DOB with a deadline: it must reach a
sounding's samples before the ringbuffer overwrites them. Measured over 1333
soundings on 2026-08-11 it missed 57 of them -- **4.28% of the archive, lost
silently** as "missing data - skipping" in ionograms.log, with a median
lateness of 171 s and a worst case of 653 s.

It was running as a single process.

    93.8  python3 -u detect_chirps.py      <- rank 0
    93.7  python3 -u detect_chirps.py      <- rank 1
    81.9  python3 -u calc_ionograms.py     <- one process, no mpirun

Eight cores, ~2.7 busy. The consumer that could not keep up had one of them
while five sat idle.

It is MPI-aware already -- `from mpi4py import MPI` at calc_ionograms.py:8,
`Get_size`/`Get_rank` at :100-101 -- and the configuration format says so too:
`sounder_timings` in the ini is a list of lists, **one schedule per rank**.
That structure only exists because this script is meant to run distributed.
Line 122 already launches `detect_chirps.py` this way; this is the same change
for the process that actually needed it.

**-np 2, and this number was paid for.** The first version of this patch said
4, from a `ps` snapshot showing detect_chirps at 94+94 and calc_ionograms at
82 -- about 2.7 of 8 cores, apparently five idle. That reading counted the top
three processes and ignored the five digisonde receivers, two plotters,
find_timings and the ringbuffer pruner. Load average was already ~10.

With four ranks the machine ran at load 11.4 and the recorder lost **1155 s of
signal in 1835 s of uptime -- 63% of the stream**, as 2.2 M drop events and
2.1 M empty receives in thor.log. Reverting to two: 0.00 s lost.

A late ionogram is recoverable; a dropped sample is gone forever. The producer
outranks the consumer, and any change here must be checked with `uptime` and
the drop counters, not with a `ps` snapshot:

    grep -oE 'samp_diff [0-9]+' logs/thor.log | awk '{s+=$2} END {printf "%.2f s lost\n", s/25e6}'
    ps -o etimes= -p $(pgrep -f rx_uhd_ext_gps)

Memory was never the constraint: 437 MB per rank against 11 GB available.

**This does not fix a slow write path.** The same measurement was taken with
products going to a 5400 rpm laptop disk over ntfs-3g. Parallelism divides the
compute; it does not make a seek-bound filesystem faster, and four ranks
writing to one spindle may contend. Re-measure after applying:

    grep -oE '\-?[0-9.]+ s left' logs/find_timings.log \
      | awk '{n++; if($1<=0) z++} END {printf "n=%d lost=%d (%.2f%%)\n", n, z+0, 100*z/n}'

Baseline to beat: 4.28%.

Apply on top of 0003, which this context assumes:

    cd ~/chirpsounder2
    git apply --check /path/to/0004-dombas-run-calc_ionograms-under-mpi.patch
    git apply         /path/to/0004-dombas-run-calc_ionograms-under-mpi.patch

Takes effect on the next launch of dombas.sh -- and only on a relaunch, since
the script starts these processes once. Never start a second calc_ionograms
beside the running one: two consumers on one ringbuffer, both writing the same
products, is worse than the fault this fixes.

diff --git a/examples/marieluise/dombas.sh b/examples/marieluise/dombas.sh
--- a/examples/marieluise/dombas.sh
+++ b/examples/marieluise/dombas.sh
@@ -137,7 +137,11 @@ if [ "$SERENDIPITOUS" = "True" ]; then
 fi
 
 echo "calc_ionograms.py"
-python3 -u calc_ionograms.py --config "$CONF_FILE" > logs/ionograms.log 2>&1 &
+# Under MPI: this is the process with the ringbuffer deadline, and as a single
+# process it lost 4.28% of soundings. Two ranks, not four -- at four the
+# machine saturated and the RECORDER lost 63% of its stream. It reads
+# its rank from MPI.COMM_WORLD (calc_ionograms.py:99-101), and `sounder_timings`
+# is stored one list per rank for exactly this reason.
+$MPIRUN -np 2 python3 -u calc_ionograms.py --config "$CONF_FILE" > logs/ionograms.log 2>&1 &
 
 echo "plot_ionograms.py"
 python3 -u plot_ionograms.py --config "$CONF_FILE" > logs/plot_ionograms.log 2>&1 &
